\nAlso handle fn returning coroutine — fn is async fn so fn(item) returns coroutine; ensure_future schedules it. Good.\n\nShould I also support async iterables? Task says \"any iterable\" — sync iterables cover lists, generators, sets, dicts, strings. I'll keep to sync iterables.\n\nWhat about limit being e.g. numpy int? Overthinking. Keep simple.\n\nAlso, if items is a plain list, `iter(items)` fine.\n\nEdge: exception ordering — if head task completes with exception but a later-index task also completed with result, we might raise before recording result — fine, we're raising anyway.\n\nOne more: what if `fn` raises synchronously when called (before coroutine creation)? fn is async, so calling it always returns a coroutine; exceptions happen inside. But if fn is any callable returning awaitable, fn(item) could raise synchronously — then next() loop... the raise would propagate out of bounded_map, with the except cancelling pending tasks. Good, robust.\n\nAlso consider: what if the same coroutine... no.\n\nCancellation: if bounded_map is cancelled while awaiting asyncio.wait, CancelledError raised, except clause cancels children and awaits them, then re-raises. Awaiting gather inside except while itself cancelled — in Python 3.8+, after CancelledError, awaiting gather may immediately raise CancelledError again? Actually in a cancelled task, subsequent awaits can proceed unless cancellation is re-requested. gather(*pending, return_exceptions=True) with tasks being cancelled — it will wait for them. There's a subtlety: if the outer task is being cancelled, awaiting inside except can raise CancelledError if another cancel arrives. To be safe, use `await asyncio.gather(...)` inside try? Common pattern is fine. Alternatively shield. I'll use uncancel-agnostic simple approach — standard pattern in asyncio docs (e.g., similar to asyncio.as_completed / TaskGroup behavior). Actually Python 3.11 TaskGroup would be cleaner but changes semantics slightly (raises ExceptionGroup