othing\n\n    try:\n        while True:\n            # refill\n            while len(pending) < limit and not exhausted:\n                try:\n                    item = next(iterator)\n                except StopIteration:\n                    exhausted = True\n                    break\n                index = len(results)\n                results.append(None)\n                task = asyncio.ensure_future(fn(item))\n                pending[task] = index\n            if not pending:\n                break\n            done, _ = await asyncio.wait(pending, return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED)\n            for task in done:\n                index = pending.pop(task)\n                results[index] = task.result()  # raises on exception\n    except BaseException:\n        for task in pending:\n            task.cancel()\n        if pending:\n            await asyncio.gather(*pending, return_exceptions=True)\n        raise\n    return results\n```\n\nNote: `task.result()` raises if the task raised — good, propagates first completed exception. But wait: if multiple done, and one raised, we pop it and call result() which raises, leaving other done tasks' results uncollected and other pending tasks not cancelled — but the except clause cancels all remaining tasks in `pending` dict (the ones not popped). Done-but-not-popped tasks remain in pending dict too (they were in `done` but the loop raised before popping them) — they're completed so cancel() is a no-op, and gather with return_exceptions=True retrieves their exceptions. Good.\n\nOne subtlety: `asyncio.wait` with a dict — passing dict iterates keys, fine. But asyncio.wait requires tasks, not coroutines — we use ensure_future, good.\n\nAnother subtlety: eager task creation — we only create tasks as window slots free, so bounded. Good.\n\nAlso `asyncio.wait` deprecation: passing coroutines deprecated, tasks fine. In Python 3.12+, asyncio.wait with an empty set raises ValueError — we guard with `if not pending: break`.\n