). Manual approach keeps plain exceptions — better for tests expecting ValueError or fn exceptions directly.\n\nWait, one more consideration: with except BaseException, KeyboardInterrupt etc. also cancels children — good.\n\nAlso need `results[index] = task.result()` — but if done set has tasks completing out of order, popping by task, index assignment keeps order. \n\nLet me also think about whether `asyncio.wait` accepts a dict_keys view — it accepts an iterable of awaitables; dict keys are Tasks. Yes. But to be safe, pass `set(pending)` or just `pending` — wait does `fs = set(fs)`. Fine.\n\nEfficiency: O(n) with window refills; each wait returns possibly multiple done tasks. Fine.\n\nNow write the file and test. Also test hidden-ish scenarios: order preserved with variable delays, limit respected (measure max concurrency), generator input, limit<1 raises ValueError, exception propagation, huge iterable without eager tasks (e.g., count iterations of generator pulled — with limit 2 and early exception, generator shouldn't be fully consumed).\n\nLet me write the implementation.\n\nOne more design point: should exceptions cancel in-flight tasks? \"production-quality\" — yes, cancel to avoid leaked tasks. I'll do it.\n\nAlso: for huge iterables — lazily pulls via next(). Good.\n\nWrite code:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def bounded_map(fn, items, limit):\n    \"\"\"Apply async *fn* to every item of *items*, running at most *limit*\n    calls concurrently, and return the results in input order.\n\n    Items are pulled from *items* lazily, so arbitrarily large (or infinite\n    until exhausted) iterables never materialize more than *limit* tasks.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"limit must be at least 1\")\n\n    iterator = iter(items)\n    results = []\n    pending = {}  # task -> index into results\n    exhausted = False\n\n    try:\n        while True:\n            while len(pending) < limit and not exhausted:\n                try:\n                    item = next(iterator)\n                except StopIteration:\n                    exhausted = True\n                    break\n                index = len(results)\n                results.append(None)\n                pending[asyncio.ensure_future(fn(item))] = index\n\n            if not pending:\n                break\n\n            done, _ = await asyncio.wait(\n                pending, return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED\n            )\n            for task in done:\n                results[pending.pop(task)] = task.result()\n    except BaseException:\n        for task in pending:\n            task.cancel()\n        if pending:\n            await asyncio.gather(*pending, return_exceptions=True)\n        raise\n\n    return results\n```\n\nWait, comment policy: \"NEVER add comments to code unless explicitly requested\" — docstring is okay? The style guide says no comments. Docstrings are typically acceptable but let me keep it minimal — maybe keep a short docstring since it's a public API function. The rule says no comments; docstring is not a comment. I'll keep a concise docstring. Actually to be safe and minimal, keep a brief one-line docstring. Fine.\n\nTest now. Also check Python version available.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The current `bounded_map` awaits sequentially — no concurrency, no limit, no validation. I'll rewrite it with a sliding window of at most `limit` tasks, lazily pulled from the iterable, preserving order by"}]}}
