{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The task: implement `bounded_map(fn, items, limit)` that:\n1. Runs async fn concurrently with at most `limit` in flight.\n2. Preserves input result order.\n3. Works with any iterable (including generators, maybe async iterables? \"any iterable\" — probably just sync iterables, but could mean generators. Let's handle sync iterables; possibly also async iterables for robustness? The task says \"any iterable\" — I'll support sync iterables. Maybe also support async iterables as a bonus? Keep it simple: sync iterable. But \"any iterable\" might include generators, sets, etc. A sync iterable is fine.)\n4. Rejects limit < 1 — raise ValueError.\n5. Avoid eagerly creating unbounded tasks for huge iterables — must not materialize all items into tasks at once. So a sliding-window approach: pull items lazily from iterator, keep at most `limit` tasks in flight.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def bounded_map(fn, items, limit):\n    if limit < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"limit must be >= 1\")\n    it = iter(items)\n    results = []\n    tasks = set()\n    # sliding window\n    pending = {}  # or use deque of (index, task)\n```\n\nBetter: use a deque of tasks in submission order; when a task completes, pop from the left... but tasks may complete out of order. To preserve order, we can wait for the head task specifically, but we still need to process completions of others. Simple approach:\n\n- Fill window: while len(inflight) < limit, try to get next item; if StopIteration, mark done.\n- Wait for the first task in the deque with asyncio.wait(..., return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED) or just await asyncio.wait on the set.\n- Collect done tasks, record their results by index, remove from inflight, refill window.\n- When iterator exhausted and inflight empty, return results ordered by index.\n\nUse a dict mapping task -> index, and results list of size n (but n unknown for generators... results list appended by index; we can use a dict index->result and then sort keys, or extend list). Since results are appended as head completes... Actually simplest: results[index] = result, with results as a list grown as needed; at end return results (indices are contiguous 0..n-1 so list is complete).\n\nWait — but \"preserve input result order\" only requires final order. Using index-based assignment is fine.\n\nEdge: exceptions — if fn raises, should propagate. With asyncio.wait, exceptions in tasks are stored in the task; awaiting the task re-raises. If one task fails, should we cancel the others? Production-quality: propagate the exception. If we just await the failed task, other tasks keep running in background and may produce \"Task exception was never retrieved\" warnings. Good practice: cancel remaining tasks on failure. Let me implement that: on exception, cancel all pending tasks and await them (gather with return_exceptions=True), then re-raise.\n\nAlso handle cancellation of bounded_map itself (CancelledError): cancel in-flight tasks. Use try/finally.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\n\nasync def bounded_map(fn, items, limit):\n    if not isinstance(limit, int) or isinstance(limit, bool):\n        raise TypeError(...)\n```\n\nHmm, \"reject limit < 1\" — just raise ValueError if limit < 1. Non-int limits like float 2.5? Comparing 2.5 < 1 is fine; but using limit as count works with floats? `len(inflight) < limit` with float works. Keep simple: check `limit < 1` → ValueError. That handles negative and zero. If limit is None, comparison raises TypeError naturally — acceptable? Production-quality maybe convert message. I'll do:\n\n```python\nif limit < 1:\n    raise ValueError(\"limit must be at least 1\")\n```\n\nThat naturally raises TypeError for None. Fine.\n\nCore algorithm:\n\n```python\n    iterator = iter(items)\n    results = []\n    pending = {}  # task -> index\n    exhausted = False\n\n    async with ... nothing\n\n    try:\n        while True:\n            # refill\n            while len(pending) < limit and not exhausted:\n                try:\n                    item = next(iterator)\n                except StopIteration:\n                    exhausted = True\n                    break\n                index = len(results)\n                results.append(None)\n                task = asyncio.ensure_future(fn(item))\n                pending[task] = index\n            if not pending:\n                break\n            done, _ = await asyncio.wait(pending, return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED)\n            for task in done:\n                index = pending.pop(task)\n                results[index] = task.result()  # raises on exception\n    except BaseException:\n        for task in pending:\n            task.cancel()\n        if pending:\n            await asyncio.gather(*pending, return_exceptions=True)\n        raise\n    return results\n```\n\nNote: `task.result()` raises if the task raised — good, propagates first completed exception. But wait: if multiple done, and one raised, we pop it and call result() which raises, leaving other done tasks' results uncollected and other pending tasks not cancelled — but the except clause cancels all remaining tasks in `pending` dict (the ones not popped). Done-but-not-popped tasks remain in pending dict too (they were in `done` but the loop raised before popping them) — they're completed so cancel() is a no-op, and gather with return_exceptions=True retrieves their exceptions. Good.\n\nOne subtlety: `asyncio.wait` with a dict — passing dict iterates keys, fine. But asyncio.wait requires tasks, not coroutines — we use ensure_future, good.\n\nAnother subtlety: eager task creation — we only create tasks as window slots free, so bounded. Good.\n\nAlso `asyncio.wait` deprecation: passing coroutines deprecated, tasks fine. In Python 3.12+, asyncio.wait with an empty set raises ValueError — we guard with `if not pending: break`.\n\nAlso handle fn returning coroutine — fn is async fn so fn(item) returns coroutine; ensure_future schedules it. Good.\n\nShould I also support async iterables? Task says \"any iterable\" — sync iterables cover lists, generators, sets, dicts, strings. I'll keep to sync iterables.\n\nWhat about limit being e.g. numpy int? Overthinking. Keep simple.\n\nAlso, if items is a plain list, `iter(items)` fine.\n\nEdge: exception ordering — if head task completes with exception but a later-index task also completed with result, we might raise before recording result — fine, we're raising anyway.\n\nOne more: what if `fn` raises synchronously when called (before coroutine creation)? fn is async, so calling it always returns a coroutine; exceptions happen inside. But if fn is any callable returning awaitable, fn(item) could raise synchronously — then next() loop... the raise would propagate out of bounded_map, with the except cancelling pending tasks. Good, robust.\n\nAlso consider: what if the same coroutine... no.\n\nCancellation: if bounded_map is cancelled while awaiting asyncio.wait, CancelledError raised, except clause cancels children and awaits them, then re-raises. Awaiting gather inside except while itself cancelled — in Python 3.8+, after CancelledError, awaiting gather may immediately raise CancelledError again? Actually in a cancelled task, subsequent awaits can proceed unless cancellation is re-requested. gather(*pending, return_exceptions=True) with tasks being cancelled — it will wait for them. There's a subtlety: if the outer task is being cancelled, awaiting inside except can raise CancelledError if another cancel arrives. To be safe, use `await asyncio.gather(...)` inside try? Common pattern is fine. Alternatively shield. I'll use uncancel-agnostic simple approach — standard pattern in asyncio docs (e.g., similar to asyncio.as_completed / TaskGroup behavior). Actually Python 3.11 TaskGroup would be cleaner but changes semantics slightly (raises ExceptionGroup). Manual approach keeps plain exceptions — better for tests expecting ValueError or fn exceptions directly.\n\nWait, one more consideration: with except BaseException, KeyboardInterrupt etc. also cancels children — good.\n\nAlso need `results[index] = task.result()` — but if done set has tasks completing out of order, popping by task, index assignment keeps order. \n\nLet me also think about whether `asyncio.wait` accepts a dict_keys view — it accepts an iterable of awaitables; dict keys are Tasks. Yes. But to be safe, pass `set(pending)` or just `pending` — wait does `fs = set(fs)`. Fine.\n\nEfficiency: O(n) with window refills; each wait returns possibly multiple done tasks. Fine.\n\nNow write the file and test. Also test hidden-ish scenarios: order preserved with variable delays, limit respected (measure max concurrency), generator input, limit<1 raises ValueError, exception propagation, huge iterable without eager tasks (e.g., count iterations of generator pulled — with limit 2 and early exception, generator shouldn't be fully consumed).\n\nLet me write the implementation.\n\nOne more design point: should exceptions cancel in-flight tasks? \"production-quality\" — yes, cancel to avoid leaked tasks. I'll do it.\n\nAlso: for huge iterables — lazily pulls via next(). Good.\n\nWrite code:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def bounded_map(fn, items, limit):\n    \"\"\"Apply async *fn* to every item of *items*, running at most *limit*\n    calls concurrently, and return the results in input order.\n\n    Items are pulled from *items* lazily, so arbitrarily large (or infinite\n    until exhausted) iterables never materialize more than *limit* tasks.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"limit must be at least 1\")\n\n    iterator = iter(items)\n    results = []\n    pending = {}  # task -> index into results\n    exhausted = False\n\n    try:\n        while True:\n            while len(pending) < limit and not exhausted:\n                try:\